## Review summary

The lifecycle-manager half of this fix is correct and complete. The session-manager half has a gap introduced by a PR that merged after this draft was opened.

---

### What works

**`lifecycle-status-decisions.ts`** — the core change is right. Replacing `createLifecycleDecision({ status: KILLED, sessionState: \"terminated\" })` with `createDetectingDecision(...)` in the `dead+dead+!recentActivity` branch correctly routes single-shot probe misses through detecting instead of a terminal state. The attempt counter is preserved, so the standard `detecting → stuck` escalation still fires after `DETECTING_MAX_ATTEMPTS`. The lifecycle-manager tests (dead→detecting, detecting→revive→working) cover the important paths.

---

### The gap — persistence block in `sm.list()` (added by #1466)

PR #1466 (storage redesign, merged 2026-04-28) added a new block in `session-manager.ts` that runs immediately after `enrichSessionWithRuntimeState`:

```typescript
// session-manager.ts ~line 1911
// Persist lifecycle to disk when enrichment detected a dead runtime.
if (
  session.lifecycle &&
  (session.lifecycle.runtime.state === "missing" || ...) &&
  session.lifecycle.session.state !== "terminated" &&   // "detecting" passes this guard
  session.lifecycle.session.state !== "done"
) {
  const persisted = buildUpdatedLifecycle(sessionName, raw, (next) => {
    next.session.state = "terminated";    // ← overwrites the "detecting" set by enrichment
    next.session.reason = "runtime_lost";
    next.session.terminatedAt = new Date().toISOString();
  });
  session.lifecycle = persisted;           // in-memory also reverts to "terminated"
  session.status = deriveLegacyStatus(persisted); // → "killed"
}
```

This block didn't exist when the PR was drafted. In production on current `main`, the flow is:

1. `enrichSessionWithRuntimeState` → sets in-memory `lifecycle.session.state = "detecting"` ✓ (PR's fix)
2. Persistence block fires — `"detecting" !== "terminated"` passes the guard → persists **`"terminated"`** to disk
3. `session.lifecycle = persisted` → in-memory also becomes `"terminated"`
4. `session.status = deriveLegacyStatus(persisted)` → `"killed"`

Net result: the `session-manager.ts` change in this PR is a no-op in production. The SM path still latches to terminated on a single dead probe.

The PR's `query.test.ts` assertions pass despite this because `buildUpdatedLifecycle` requires a JSON metadata file on disk in the format introduced by #1466. On the PR's branch (pre-#1466), the persistence block doesn't exist so the test exercises the correct path. After rebasing, the test will fail and expose the gap.

---

### Required fix

In the persistence block, persist `"detecting"` instead of `"terminated"`:

```typescript
const persisted = buildUpdatedLifecycle(sessionName, raw, (next) => {
  next.session.state = "detecting";           // not "terminated"
  next.session.reason = "runtime_lost";
  next.session.lastTransitionAt = new Date().toISOString(); // no terminatedAt
  next.runtime.state = session.lifecycle!.runtime.state;
  next.runtime.reason = session.lifecycle!.runtime.reason;
  next.runtime.lastObservedAt = new Date().toISOString();
});
```

`deriveLegacyStatus(persisted)` will return `"detecting"`, consistent with what `enrichSessionWithRuntimeState` set. Add a test that reads the raw on-disk metadata after `sm.list()` and asserts `session.state === "detecting"` and no `terminatedAt` — the existing `query.test.ts` tests don't exercise the persistence path so this gap went unnoticed.

---

### Summary

| Area | Status |
|------|--------|
| `lifecycle-status-decisions.ts` fix | Complete |
| `lifecycle-manager.test.ts` tests | Complete |
| `session-manager.ts` enrichment change | Correct in isolation |
| `session-manager.ts` persistence block (~line 1911, added by #1466) | **Not fixed — undoes the enrichment change in production** |
| Needs rebase onto current `main` | Yes |

Two things needed before this can merge: rebase onto `main` (which will surface the conflict with the persistence block), fix the persistence block to write `"detecting"` instead of `"terminated"`, and add a test that pins the on-disk state.

